0 Single-Cylinder Engine
0 Name: engine.ldr
0 Author: erik-larsen
0 // A crank turning on an axle drives a piston up and down its cylinder through a connecting
0 // rod. The crank's pin is half a stud from its axle, so the piston's stroke is one stud
0 // (20 LDU). Shown at top dead centre: crank pin straight above the axle, piston at the top.
0 // The frame is two 1x4 Technic bricks with two 1x4 plates between, holding the cylinder head
0 // on two pins and the crank's axle. Everything that moves is in front of the bricks (z = 0 to 20).

0 // Step 1: frame
1 14 0 16 30 -1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 -1 3701.dat
1 0 0 40 30 -1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 -1 3710.dat
1 0 0 48 30 -1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 -1 3710.dat
1 14 0 56 30 -1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 -1 3701.dat
0 STEP

0 // Step 2: the cylinder head, pinned to the upper brick
1 0 -20 26 20 0 0 -1 0 1 0 1 0 0 3673.dat
1 0 20 26 20 0 0 -1 0 1 0 1 0 0 3673.dat
1 72 0 0 0 -1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 -1 2850a.dat
0 STEP

0 // Step 3: the crank on a 2L axle through the lower brick, its pin pointing up
1 0 0 66 30 0 0 -1 0 1 0 1 0 0 3704.dat
1 71 0 66 0 0 1 0 1 0 0 0 0 -1 2853a.dat
0 STEP

0 // Step 4: the connecting rod on the crank pin, and the piston on its towball, in the cylinder
1 0 0 56 0 -1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 -1 2852.dat
1 71 0 16 0 -1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 -1 2851.dat
0 STEP
